# The ONE home for the digest of every upstream daemon tarball this repository's
# images install. Rule 8: a second copy of a digest is a bug with a delay on it.
#
# Role: build-input pin (data, consumed by `sha256sum -c`)
# Reads: nothing
# Writes: nothing
# Can move funds: no. It decides which BYTES become a regtest daemon; it never
#        signs, broadcasts or reads a credential.
# Mainnet-safe: yes. Nothing here is network-specific, and the image that consumes
#        it runs regtest only (regtest/daemons.assert_regtest() is unconditional).
#
# FORMAT IS `sha256sum -c`'s OWN, deliberately, so this repository writes no parser
# of its own. tests/test_docker_build_context.py's header argues the same thing
# about not reimplementing docker's ignore matcher: the moment our parser and the
# real tool disagree, the test passes while the build ships something else.
#
# MEASURED 2026-11-21, GNU coreutils 9.4 in this container, so the prose above is
# not a guess about what the tool tolerates:
#   - `#` comment lines and blank lines: accepted SILENTLY, no "improperly
#     formatted" warning, rc=0. That is why this header can live in the file the
#     tool reads instead of in a second file beside it.
#   - a 63-hex (one character short) digest line: SKIPPED with a warning, and the
#     run still exits 0 if any OTHER line verified. So a typo here DISARMS half the
#     check silently, which is why
#     tests/test_upstream_daemons_are_pinned_and_verified.py asserts every line is
#     exactly 64 lowercase hex rather than trusting the tool to complain.
#   - a manifest that pins no filename the build downloaded:
#     "sha256sum: <file>: no file was verified", rc=1. THAT is what makes
#     docker/harness.Dockerfile's `--ignore-missing` fail-closed: bump
#     BITCOIN_VERSION without adding a line here and the BUILD refuses, not just a
#     test.
#
# NOT MEASURED: debian:bookworm-slim ships coreutils 9.1, and all three behaviors
# above were measured on 9.4 here. `--ignore-missing` has existed since coreutils
# 8.25, so the flag is present; its zero-files-verified exit status on 9.1 is
# REASONED from 9.4's, not observed. Re-measure it on a host with a docker daemon
# before trusting the fail-closed claim -- that is the first item in this change's
# operator hand-off.
#
# WHY THIS FILE AND NOT upstream's SHA256SUMS FETCHED AT BUILD TIME. A manifest
# pulled from the same host over the same channel as the tarball verifies TRANSPORT
# and nothing else: it catches corruption, not substitution. A digest reviewed once
# and committed here is trust-on-first-use and detects a changed artifact forever.
#
# HOW A LINE GETS ADDED, AND THIS PARAGRAPH IS THE ONLY ENFORCEMENT THERE IS. No
# test can tell a correct digest from the digest of whatever was downloaded last
# Tuesday -- a digest is a digest. So the failure mode to refuse by hand is: a
# version bump breaks the build, and the fastest green is
# `sha256sum thetarball >> this file`. That records the digest of an UNVERIFIED
# artifact and calls it a pin. A new line is added only after the project's own
# signed release has been checked:
#
#     Bitcoin Core    https://bitcoincore.org/bin/bitcoin-core-<v>/SHA256SUMS
#                     plus SHA256SUMS.asc, against the Bitcoin Core builder keys
#                     (github.com/bitcoin-core/guix.sigs)
#     Litecoin Core   https://download.litecoin.org/litecoin-<v>/SHA256SUMS.asc,
#                     which is CLEARSIGNED -- the digests and the signature are one
#                     document -- against the release key
#
# =============================================================================
# PROVENANCE OF EACH LINE BELOW, measured 2026-11-21 from this container. The two
# projects are NOT equally well attested and writing all four lines in one voice
# would be rule 17's exact failure: presenting a hypothesis in the register of a
# measurement.
# =============================================================================
#
# BITCOIN, both lines: UPSTREAM'S OWN, copied from
# https://bitcoincore.org/bin/bitcoin-core-28.1/SHA256SUMS (HTTP 200, 2620 bytes).
# The x86_64 line is additionally confirmed by outcome -- the 49,598,812-byte
# tarball was downloaded here and `sha256sum --ignore-missing -c` against that
# manifest printed "bitcoin-28.1-x86_64-linux-gnu.tar.gz: OK", rc=0.
# NOT VERIFIED: the PGP signature. bitcoincore.org's SHA256SUMS is unsigned plain
# text (zero "BEGIN PGP" matches) and the 13 detached signatures in SHA256SUMS.asc
# all fail with "No public key" -- the builder keys live in a GitHub repository this
# session's proxy blocks, and no keyserver is reachable on 443. So Bitcoin's chain
# here is TLS plus a checksum file from the same host: transit integrity, not
# provenance. The operator's one-time check closes that.
#
# LITECOIN, both lines: ALSO UPSTREAM'S OWN, AND PGP-SIGNED. This corrects the
# design note this change was written from, which reported that
# download.litecoin.org publishes no checksum manifest and that the Litecoin
# digests were therefore locally computed. It does publish one, at the RELEASE
# ROOT rather than under linux/ or src/:
#   https://download.litecoin.org/litecoin-0.21.4/SHA256SUMS.asc  HTTP 200, 1775 bytes
# It is a CLEARSIGNED document covering all NINE release artifacts, both linux
# tarballs included. Earlier probes went to litecoin-0.21.4/linux/SHA256SUMS and
# litecoin-0.21.4/src/SHA256SUMS, which 404 -- one directory level too deep -- and
# "no manifest exists" was concluded from "not at the path I tried". `gpg --verify`
# on it: "Good signature from David Burkett <davidburkett38@gmail.com>", RSA key
# D35621D53A1CC6A3456758D03620E9D387E55666, signature made 2024-10-22. The x86_64
# line is also confirmed by outcome against the 36,737,533-byte tarball
# ("litecoin-0.21.4-x86_64-linux-gnu.tar.gz: OK", rc=0).
# CAVEAT, AND IT IS NOT SMALL: the key was fetched from download.litecoin.org --
# the same host that serves the artifact it signs. That establishes the release's
# internal self-consistency, NOT independent attestation; a compromised host could
# have replaced tarball, manifest and key as a set. gpg says so itself: "This key
# is not certified with a trusted signature." No keyserver is reachable from here
# to cross-check the fingerprint.
#
# NEITHER aarch64 TARBALL WAS DOWNLOADED HERE. Both aarch64 digests come from the
# upstream manifests above and nothing else. That is the same evidence the x86_64
# lines rest on minus the local recomputation, and it is stated rather than rounded
# up to "verified".
#
# UPSTREAM PUBLISHES MORE ARCHES THAN THESE FOUR LINES COVER, and that is correct
# rather than an omission: docker/harness.Dockerfile's `case "$arch"` builds for
# amd64 and arm64 only and exits 1 on anything else.
# tests/test_upstream_daemons_are_pinned_and_verified.py derives the arch list from
# that case block, so adding an arm (bitcoin publishes arm-linux-gnueabihf, riscv64
# and powerpc64; litecoin publishes arm-linux-gnueabihf and riscv64) without adding
# its digest here fails at suite time with the filename named -- instead of failing
# only on somebody else's machine, in a build log, as coreutils' bare "no file was
# verified".

